{
  "module": "Training: how the numbers are learned",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "next-token-loss",
    "title": "The pretraining loss and perplexity",
    "summary": "The loss a language model is trained on: how much probability it gave the true next token at each position, averaged, and perplexity, the same number read as an effective number of choices.",
    "covers": ["pretraining-loss"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "training-basics",
        "reason": "This is a particular loss, so what a loss is must already be clear."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "next-token-sampling",
        "reason": "The loss is computed from the next-token distribution the model produces."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "goal",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A language model is trained to predict the next token. For a piece of real text, at every position we know the "
            },
            {
              "text": "true next token",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", because it is right there in the text."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The model gives a probability to every token in the vocabulary. A good model gives a "
            },
            {
              "text": "high probability to the true one",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 72)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "minus-log",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The loss at one position looks only at the probability "
            },
            {
              "text": "P",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " the model gave to the true next token, and turns it into an error: "
            },
            {
              "text": "−log P",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", using the natural logarithm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "P = 0.9: loss 0.105"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "P = 0.5: loss 0.693"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "P = 0.1: loss 2.303"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "P = 0.01: loss 4.605"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A high probability on the true token gives a small loss. A probability close to 0 gives a large loss. Probability 1 would give a loss of exactly 0."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 72)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "average",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The "
            },
            {
              "text": "pretraining loss",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is that error averaged over all the n positions of the text:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "L = -\\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{t} \\log P(x_{t+1} \\mid x_1, \\ldots, x_t)",
          "spoken": "L equals minus one over n, times the sum over t of the log of the probability of x at t plus 1, given x 1 up to x t"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Each term is the probability the model gave to the true next token at position t, given the text up to that position."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 72)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "worked",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy text of three positions, where the model gave the true next token these invented probabilities: 0.5, 0.25, 0.125."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Errors: 0.693, 1.386, 2.079"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Average: (0.693 + 1.386 + 2.079) / 3 = 1.386"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Training adjusts the parameters to push this number down, which means giving the true next tokens higher probabilities."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 72)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "perplexity",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Perplexity",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is the loss turned back into something easier to picture: perplexity = exp(L)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It is the effective number of equally likely tokens the model is choosing between at each step. In the toy text, exp(1.386) = 4: the model is about as unsure as if it were picking among 4 equally likely tokens."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A check: if the model were exactly evenly torn between 4 tokens, the true one would get probability 0.25 every time. The loss would be 1.386, and exp of that is 4."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 72)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "reading",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Lower loss and lower perplexity both mean a better model. A perplexity of 1 would mean the model is completely sure of the true next token at every position, which is a loss of 0."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Loss and perplexity say the same thing in different units. The loss is what gets minimised. Perplexity is the friendlier way to read it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 72)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "What does the pretraining loss measure?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The error in the probability the model gave to the true next token, averaged over the positions of the text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The error at a position is −log of that probability."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The probability of the most likely token at each position"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The loss looks at the true next token, not at the model's favourite."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The number of tokens in the text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "That is n, which only appears as the number of positions being averaged."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The fraction of positions where the model's top guess was right"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The loss uses the probability given to the true token, not only whether it was the top guess."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "teaches": ["goal", "average"],
      "covers": ["pretraining-loss"],
      "variants": [
